\section{The model}

[[AppModel]] holds the state the views share. It imports the foveated library for the platform:
the system framework on visionOS, Apple's package on iOS. Both define a type named
[[FoveatedStreamingSession]] with the same interface, which is what lets most of the code ignore
the platform. [[SavedSettings]] keeps the selected application and the selected backend in user
defaults.

<<CloudXRViewer/Common/AppModel.swift>>=
<<NVIDIA license header>>

import SwiftUI
import RealityKit
import CloudXRKit
#if FOVEATED_STREAMING
#if os(visionOS)
import FoveatedStreaming
#else
import StreamingSession
#endif
#endif

struct SavedSettings {
    @AppStorage("application") static var application: Application = .generic_viewer
    @AppStorage("streamingBackend") static var backend: StreamingBackend = .classic
}

@
The model is [[@Observable]], so views that read its properties are refreshed when they
change, and [[@MainActor]], because [[FoveatedStreamingSession]] is main-actor isolated and the
model creates one as a stored property. The classic [[session]] is created on the first classic
connection, as in NVIDIA's code.

<<CloudXRViewer/Common/AppModel.swift>>=
/// The data that the app uses to configure its views.
@MainActor
@Observable
public class AppModel {
    /// The CloudXRKit session, created on the first classic connection.
    public var session: Session?

#if FOVEATED_STREAMING
    /// The foveated streaming session. It lives as long as the app because the visionOS
    /// `ImmersiveSpace(foveatedStreaming:)` scene is built from it.
    let foveatedSession = FoveatedStreamingSession()
#endif

@
The saved backend is honored only if this build has it; a simulator build that finds
[[foveated]] in its defaults falls back to [[classic]].

<<CloudXRViewer/Common/AppModel.swift>>=
    var backend = StreamingBackend.available.contains(SavedSettings.backend) ? SavedSettings.backend : .classic {
        didSet {
            SavedSettings.backend = backend
        }
    }

@
\label{sec:active}[[activeSession]] is the
heart of the backend switch: the session of the selected backend seen through the
[[ViewerSession]] protocol. The classic session is wrapped in a fresh [[ClassicSession]] on every
access; the wrapper holds no state, so this costs nothing and keeps SwiftUI's observation of the
underlying CloudXRKit session intact. The foveated session conforms to the protocol directly.
[[status]] and [[connectionState]] are what most views observe.

<<CloudXRViewer/Common/AppModel.swift>>=
    /// The session of the selected backend, if it exists.
    var activeSession: (any ViewerSession)? {
        switch backend {
        case .classic:
            session.map { ClassicSession($0) }
        case .foveated:
#if FOVEATED_STREAMING
            foveatedSession
#else
            nil
#endif
        }
    }

    var status: ViewerStatus {
        activeSession?.viewerStatus ?? .initialized
    }

    var connectionState: ConnectionState {
        ConnectionState(backend: backend, status: status)
    }

@
A failed foveated connection may throw before the session reports any status; the error
is kept here so the form can show it. [[disconnect()]] disconnects whichever session is active.

<<CloudXRViewer/Common/AppModel.swift>>=
    /// Why the last foveated connection attempt failed before the session reported a status.
    var foveatedConnectError: String?

    func disconnect() async {
        await activeSession?.disconnect()
    }

@
The rest of the model is NVIDIA's: feedback state, the streaming view flag (now derived
from the neutral status), the selected application and, on visionOS, the window manager.

<<CloudXRViewer/Common/AppModel.swift>>=
#if DEBUG
    var disableFeedback = true
#else
    var disableFeedback = false
#endif

    var isRatingViewPresented = false
    var ratingText = "Feedback"
    var showDisconnectionAlert = false

    var showStreamingAppView: Bool {
        status == .connected
    }

    var application = SavedSettings.application {
        didSet {
            SavedSettings.application = application
        }
    }

    #if os(visionOS)
    let windowStateManager = WindowStateManager()
    #endif
}
